Photosynthesis, also known as green chemistry or biological chemical synthesis, refers to the process of converting sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water into oxygen and glucose through plants. It is a fundamental aspect of life sciences that underlies virtually all metabolic processes.

The first recorded photosynthetic organism was cyanobacteria, which lived in marine environments around 2.5 billion years ago and still survives today. It was not until the evolution of green leaves with chlorophyll (which gives green to plants) came about in the last 40 million years that we can attribute the origin of photosynthesis as a separate biological phenomenon.

Photosynthesis, therefore, marks a major shift from simple autotrophic processes, where organisms extract energy directly from their surroundings through chemosynthesis or chemolithoautotrophy (without water), to aerobic life. It is not only a fundamental function but also an important source of energy and oxygen for the entire planet.
